 The
 .Nm
+supports TSO and TSO6, disabled by default.
+There is no wire between the two ends, so an accepted TSO chain is
+not segmented but delivered to the other end whole, up to the
+advertised chain limits.
+This removes the overhead of wire-sized segmentation for bulk TCP
+between the two ends.
+Each TSO capability requires the matching transmit checksum
+capability; disabling TXCSUM or TXCSUM6 also disables the
+corresponding TSO capability.
+Like TXCSUM, the TSO capabilities are synchronized between the
+.Nm
+interface pair.
+Only enable TSO if every path a delivered chain may take can
+terminate it, segment it in hardware, or segment it in software;
+forwarding an oversized chain to an egress interface that cannot
+segment it stalls the TCP connection on kernels without software
+TSO segmentation in the forwarding path.
+When one end is a member of an
+.Xr if_bridge 4 ,
+the bridge coordinates the capability across its members.
